1
00:00:05,300 --> 00:00:11,000
Four machines here run on industrial PCs: real J1939 buses,
Sparkplug B over MQTT, into Abenix.

2
00:00:11,083 --> 00:00:18,067
A worker walks into EX1's swing zone. The hold crosses MQTT to EX1's PC,
and EX1 is still 162 ms after the twin saw the worker.

3
00:00:18,067 --> 00:00:25,067
Every hop is measured, from the bus through the gateway
and the broker into the Abenix store, and back.

4
00:00:25,067 --> 00:00:31,083
The worker is clear. EX1 works on the fallback
plan until a person signs the stop off.

5
00:00:31,083 --> 00:00:39,000
We unplug EX1's PC. Abenix sees it die on the broker,
and EX1's own safety PLC holds it.

6
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:47,100
Plugged back in, it is born again on
the broker and goes back to work.

7
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:55,000
A noisy harness on EX1. Error frames and
retransmits on the wire, and the bus keeps going.

8
00:00:55,783 --> 00:00:58,533
EX1's main pump starts to wear.
It works harder for the same flow, and the oil heats up.

9
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:24,433
A rule in Abenix sees EX1's oil running hot, 16% above its own normal: 64.5 °C over the
last 5 minutes against 55.7 °C the half hour before. It wakes the maintenance tech.

10
00:01:24,433 --> 00:01:42,433
It reads the last 15 minutes and the manual,
and asks to put EX1 in safe mode. A person decides.

11
00:01:42,433 --> 00:01:48,433
Approved in Abenix by Admin User,
and EX1's PC takes it 0.2 s later: Mode/Set safe, confirmed on its bus.

12
00:01:48,433 --> 00:01:56,433
In Abenix every PC is a device, with live graphs, history and the raw frames.
